˛9´Íîﬂ˝¸ü∆Ù…èe√™]\ﬁd~y;4¡◊H{"url":"/gcsl/language/inference-limits/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/inference-limits/","content":"Inference Limits. GCSL infers local values and selected present-or-future positions. It does not infer every relationship between types. Explicit annotations help you state an intended interface when a program reaches one of these limits. The following boundaries affect ordinary programs: BoundaryWhat to write An empty list has no independent element type. Give it an annotation or an expected List<T> type. A pure function does not preserve arbitrary future shapes. Write the present or future contract explicitly. Recursive impure results can retain a deferred possibility. Use Present<T> when the result must be present. General type parameters and intersections are unsupported. Write concrete structural types. There is no None value or optional-type shorthand. Represent alternatives using supported union members. Narrowing recognizes a plain name tested directly by if. Bind a field to a name before testing it. Narrowing does not establish facts after an early return. Keep uses inside the narrowed branch. A narrowed mutable name is a branch-local copy. Don't try to update the original through that copy. len and show use special call rules. Call them directly or through supported aliases, rather than storing them as ordinary function values. Expected types reach record fields, call arguments, and other supported literal contexts. An unrelated sibling expression is not a general constraint-solving mechanism. For example, annotate an empty list before combining it with independently inferred values. Warnings do not prevent execution. A union inferred from unlike branch results can produce a warning while the program remains valid. Use gcsl check to inspect the full set of errors and warnings, and gcsl run --dump-types FILE when the inferred shape is unclear. These are supported-language boundaries, not promises about a future inference engine. The checker implementation and its regression tests are the source for the behavior described here.","word_count":294,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Inference Limits"},"anchors":[]}},"word_data":{"rule":[{"position":173,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"rather":[{"position":181,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"warn":[{"position":224,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":239,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":256,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"for":[{"position":211,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":289,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"pure":[{"position":61,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"produc":[{"position":237,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"annot":[{"position":19,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":54,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":213,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"result":[{"position":78,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":88,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":235,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"can":[{"position":79,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":236,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"field":[{"position":128,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":193,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"bind":[{"position":126,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"file":[{"position":261,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"is":[{"position":104,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":154,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":205,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":266,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"ordinari":[{"position":38,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":186,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"defer":[{"position":82,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"presentt":[{"position":85,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"type":[{"position":17,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":50,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":59,"weight":24,"original_w